---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@components/PageHero.astro';

const invariants = [
  { id: 'S1',  name: 'D_KL(p) growth',              tier: 'arithmetic', status: 'FAIL', why: 'depends on the parity bias of the sieve (prime gaps almost always even).' },
  { id: 'S2',  name: 'Mod-3 transitions',           tier: 'algebraic',  status: 'PASS', why: 'pure invariant of modular dynamics — survives in language.' },
  { id: 'S3',  name: 'GFT balance',                 tier: 'arithmetic', status: 'FAIL', why: 'algebraic identity of the sieve, not reproduced outside arithmetic.' },
  { id: 'S4',  name: 'Mod-6 transitional MI',       tier: 'algebraic',  status: 'PASS', why: 'informational coupling between modular projections — domain-independent.' },
  { id: 'S5',  name: 'Spectral flatness',           tier: 'arithmetic', status: 'FAIL', why: 'spectral regularity inherited from prime structure.' },
  { id: 'S6',  name: 'D_KL(m) shape',               tier: 'arithmetic', status: 'FAIL', why: 'sieve-specific profile.' },
  { id: 'S7',  name: 'ρ(T·D_j) < 1',                 tier: 'algebraic',  status: 'PASS', why: 'linguistic analogue of v6 T6 theorem — contraction of cross-modes.' },
  { id: 'S8',  name: 'Universal τ_mix',              tier: 'algebraic',  status: 'PASS', why: 'mixing time 0.17–0.30 across all languages tested.' },
  { id: 'S9',  name: 'MI(2,3) ≈ 0',                  tier: 'algebraic',  status: 'PASS', why: 'CRT complementarity preserved for the (parity, mod 3) pair.' },
  { id: 'S11', name: 'q⁻ branch (thermal)',          tier: 'bifurcation','status': 'PASS', why: 'language lives on the thermal branch: characters = propagators, not vertex couplings.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method and PT invariants (Linguistics)"
  description="Method of the PT-Linguistics programme: inter-occurrence gaps, T_m matrices, mixture-of-geometrics, q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation, axioms P1–P7. S1–S11 table: 6/10 invariants survive (algebraic core), 4/10 fail (arithmetic periphery)."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Method"
    domain="language"
    title="Method and PT invariants"
    kicker="Six PT invariants out of ten survive in language. The four that fail depend specifically on the sieve."
    dek="The PT-Linguistics programme uses primitives from the pt_native_core.py module: per-character inter-occurrence gap extraction, T_m transition matrices, mixture-of-geometrics decomposition with q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation, persistence axioms P1–P7. This page explains the measurement chain and gives the S1–S11 table that separates the algebraic core of PT (universal) from its arithmetic periphery (prime-specific)."
    width="wide"
  />

  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 md:py-14 space-y-12">

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm mb-3">Step 1</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-3">From text to inter-occurrence gaps</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Take a text as a sequence of characters c₁, c₂, …, c_N. For each
        character c appearing at positions i₁, i₂, …, the
        <strong>gap sequence</strong> is:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 text-sm font-mono mb-3">{`G_c = { i_{k+1} − i_k : k = 1, 2, … }`}</pre>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        These gaps are the <em>PT-native observable</em>: no embedding,
        no arbitrary quantisation, no fitted parameter. The aggregated
        sequence pools all characters and yields a gap distribution on ℕ⁺.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        This transformation is the strict analogue of what PT does on
        primes: replace a sequence by its inter-distances, then project
        modulo m to reveal structure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm mb-3">Step 2</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-3">Modular projections and the T_m transition matrix</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Project the gaps onto ℤ/mℤ (residues mod m) and build the
        stochastic matrix of consecutive transitions:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 text-sm font-mono mb-3">{`T_m[i,j] = P( C_{t+1} = j | C_t = i )   where   C_t = (gap_t mod m)`}</pre>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        For prime gaps, theorem T0 forces T₃[1,1] = T₃[2,2] = 0 (forbidden
        transitions). In language, we <em>measure</em> the structural
        zeros — which are not the same — and compare the spectrum, τ_mix
        and the ρ(T·D_j) contraction against PT prediction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm mb-3">Step 3</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-3">Mixture-of-geometrics and q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        For a text, each character c has its own gap statistics,
        approximately geometric with parameter 1/μ_c. The aggregated
        distribution is therefore a <strong>mixture of geometrics</strong>,
        weighted by frequencies:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 text-sm font-mono mb-3">{`P_mix(g) = Σ_c  w_c · f_c · (1 − f_c)^(g−1)`}</pre>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        On this mixture, PT tests two branches:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        <li><strong class="text-thmblue">q⁺ = 1 − 2/μ</strong> — statistical branch (vertex, coupling).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-thmblue">q⁻ = e^(−1/μ)</strong> — thermal branch (propagator, geometry).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Result S11.</strong> Across the 19 corpora tested
        (living and dead languages, creoles, ancient manuscripts),
        <em>the q⁻ branch wins without exception</em> by a factor of 4×
        to 20×. In PT physics, this means language lives on the thermal
        branch: characters behave as <em>propagators</em>, not as
        coupling vertices. A surprising and reproducible empirical fact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-val mb-3">Main result</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-3">S1–S11 table: what survives, what breaks</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Eleven formalised tests, two families. The verdict is clear:
        <strong>6/10 PASS</strong> = algebraic core, domain-independent;
        <strong>4/10 FAIL</strong> = arithmetic periphery, prime-specific
        (S1, S3, S5, S6 depend on parity bias, sieve, or integer spectrum).
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm font-sans">
          <thead class="border-b border-stone-300 text-xs text-stone-600 uppercase">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-3">Test</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-3">Name</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-3">Family</th>
              <th class="text-center py-2 px-2">Status (lang)</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pl-3">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {invariants.map((s) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
                <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-mono text-thmblue font-bold">{s.id}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3">{s.name}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3 text-xs text-stone-600">{s.tier}</td>
                <td class="py-2 px-2 text-center">
                  <span class={s.status === 'PASS'
                    ? 'inline-block px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-bold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800'
                    : 'inline-block px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-bold bg-rose-100 text-rose-800'}>
                    {s.status}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2 pl-3 text-xs text-stone-700">{s.why}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs font-sans text-stone-500">
        The six PASS results are confirmed across the 12 languages of the
        main panel (FR, EN, ES, DE, IT, LA, SA, HT, PAP, JAM, FRO, ANG)
        and on derived corpora (medieval texts, diachronic creoles,
        Voynich, Linear A).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm mb-3">Organising principle</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-3">Robustness hierarchy R(L1) ≥ R(L2) ≥ R(L3)</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Beyond individual invariants, PT predicts a <strong>robustness
        hierarchy</strong>: three structural layers whose persistence
        decreases in strict order.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">L1 — identity / parity</strong>: the most robust layer (linguistic family detection).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">L2 — Fourier / frequency</strong>: modular structure, alphabet, vowel system.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">L3 — structural residual / semantics</strong>: the most fragile layer (lexical content, syntax, meaning).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Major result.</strong> This hierarchy holds in language
        <em>even when most individual invariants break</em>. It — more
        than any S_k taken in isolation — is the deepest organising
        principle of the programme. It is used as the interpretive grid
        for the Voynich and Linear A benchmarks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-bridge mb-3">Going further</p>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="/en/linguistique/diachronie-et-evolvabilite" class="pt-card block group">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-id">Dynamics</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-lg mt-3 mb-2 group-hover:underline">Diachrony and evolvability</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">PT evolvability measured on three diachronic Caribbean creoles and two ancient/modern pairs.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="/en/linguistique" class="pt-card block group">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm">Programme</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-lg mt-3 mb-2 group-hover:underline">Back to portal</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">PT-Linguistics programme overview.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
